I managed to reproduce the original issue, but I'm seeing differences on how `mini.files` behaves on my Windows installation regarding future comments.

With the configuration

```lua
require("mini.files").setup {
  windows = {
    preview = true,
  },
}

vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd("User", {
  pattern = "MiniFilesWindowUpdate",
  callback = function()
    MiniFiles.get_explorer_state()
  end,
})
```

doing `cd C:/` and hovering `swapfile.sys` `mini.files` correctly recognizes that the file does exists and that I do not have access to read it

<img width="1210" height="713" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/78676323-224e-4fa3-842e-7eaa0cd7772a" />

(there seems to be an issue regarding how the title is shown, probably because Windows absolute paths start with a letter, instead of `/`. I think I also found a similar issue regarding `MiniFiles.open()`, but I'll look into it and open a separated issue later)

If I then use the `h` keybind I get the error mentioned in the description of the PR

<img width="1919" height="922" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/6f1be289-1001-49d5-a43e-4cd506cabef4" />

Regarding the issue with `fs_stat`, I can confirm that `vim.uv.fs_stat('C:/swapfile.sys')` returns `nil` and `ENOENT: no such file or directory` as an error. However, it doesn't seem to be a matter of forward slashes vs backslashes since `vim.uv.fs_stat('C://swapfile.sys')` returns the correct stat table just like the version with a double backslash at the beginning. As I said before, I personally don't know if this is related to the original error as I don't even see the`-No-fs-entry-` also mentioned
